---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SEMPODS_VERSION, SEMPODS_RELEASE_URL, implementationDoc, specificationDoc } from '../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="sempods status and direction — independent versions, a shared contract"
  description="What Kotlin 0.2 provides, what the development specification requires, and which directions remain proposed or unfinished."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">status and direction</p>
  <h1>Useful today. Room to build.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The live pods demonstrate the idea. The specification defines a common
    contract. The Kotlin modules give developers a place to start. Each has
    its own state and its own next steps.
  </p>
  <h2>Available in Kotlin {SEMPODS_VERSION}</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>HTTP CRUD, read-only SPARQL, Context permissions and graph retrieval.</li>
    <li>A JVM HTTP client with separate RDF4J and media adapters.</li>
    <li>Delegated OAuth access and experimental service registration with owner consent.</li>
    <li>Per-pod MCP, a hosted multi-pod MCP service, and an OIDC identity service.</li>
    <li>Reusable modules and a local development/evaluation composition.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href={SEMPODS_RELEASE_URL}>release notes</a> record breaking changes,
    deprecations and known limitations. The <a href={implementationDoc('docs/migration/0.2.md')}>migration guide</a>{' '}covers consumers moving from 0.1. A release does not identify the version
    running on every public host.
  </p>
  <h2>A development specification with normative requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Core and optional modules are currently labeled <code>0.1-dev</code>.
    Their source commit identifies a particular revision. Their versions are
    independent of Kotlin releases and of each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The normative chapters already determine the contract. Implementation
    behavior does not override them. A product conformance suite remains
    unfinished, and Kotlin 0.2 does not yet serve the required conformance
    discovery endpoint. No certification is claimed.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">Read the development specification</a>{' '}
    <a href={specificationDoc('GOVERNANCE.md')}>Read governance</a>
  </p>
  <h2>A smaller common interface</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={specificationDoc('docs/vision.md')}>vision</a> is a framework
    that composes existing standards while leaving implementations free to
    choose their storage and policy mechanisms.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={specificationDoc('docs/proposals/data-access.md')}>Authorized data access</a>{' '}proposes ordinary access without Context setup. Context discovery and
    selection remain part of that proposed interface. Adoption is tracked in{' '}<a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-spec/issues/68">specification issue 68</a>.
    The current contract still uses Contexts as its authorization boundary.
  </p>
  <h2>Easier paths into the ecosystem</h2>
  <p>
    Better retrieval adapters, semantic app contracts and clients in more
    languages could make more use cases practical. Their public issues own
    scope and progress; a direction here promises no delivery date.
  </p>
  <p>
    Concrete distributions with fixed configurations and verified upgrade
    procedures would also make operation easier. The current composition
    supplies development building blocks and has no supported upgrade path.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/start">Use what is available</a>{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-spec/issues">Specification work</a>{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/issues">Implementation work</a>
  </p>
</Base>
